Notebook[{
Cell["Lesson 1 log \[CenterDot] Sequence to sequence with deep LSTMs", "Title",ExpressionUUID->"57a1cfa5-cdea-499f-9803-322089995b9d"],

Cell["\<\
Written 2026-11-24 01:47 UTC by lesson1_lstm_seq2seq.nb. 19 of 19 checks pass.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"44215871-c006-4b28-b440-7f619ec5772e"],

Cell["\<\
Paper: I. Sutskever, O. Vinyals, Q. V. Le, Sequence to Sequence Learning with \
Neural Networks, NeurIPS 2014, arXiv:1409.3215. Each part is coded from plain \
arrays in lesson1_lstm_seq2seq.nb and checked against Wolfram's layers. The \
trained demo nets for this paper are in ref1_seq2seq_sutskever2014.nb.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"d8f86dbf-530f-448b-a76c-4f976baac4ee"],

Cell["1. The LSTM cell", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"a483db52-f4b9-4e65-bff4-a4eb6047fd69"],

Cell["\<\
An LSTM keeps a hidden state h and a memory cell c. Each step reads one input \
vector x and computes four gates; the memory is updated by addition, which is \
what lets information and gradients survive across long sentences.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"b0c375b9-f92a-4288-a6e2-b823f3938eb8"],

Cell["\<\
i = \[Sigma](Wi\[CenterDot]x + Ui\[CenterDot]h + bi)    f = \[Sigma](Wf\
\[CenterDot]x + Uf\[CenterDot]h + bf)    o = \[Sigma](Wo\[CenterDot]x + Uo\
\[CenterDot]h + bo)
g = tanh(Wg\[CenterDot]x + Ug\[CenterDot]h + bg)
c' = f\[Times]c + i\[Times]g        h' = o\[Times]tanh(c')\
\>", "Program",ExpressionUUID->"8f7c018a-4a8d-4e48-a30b-1cd9b7d81137"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["PASS  ",
  FontWeight->Bold,
  FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0.5, 0]],
 "the cell coded from arrays gives the same hidden states as \
LongShortTermMemoryLayer (7 steps, 4 inputs, 6 cells)"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"b4bd4753-12f8-4325-b0ee-04d454e14d90"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["PASS  ",
  FontWeight->Bold,
  FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0.5, 0]],
 "it also agrees when it starts from given states h0, c0 (the decoder's \
situation)"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"c6e3b06e-c5f5-4c3e-a68c-338a07e8306c"],

Cell["\<\
In Wolfram the four gates are the arrays InputGate\[Ellipsis], ForgetGate\
\[Ellipsis], OutputGate\[Ellipsis] and MemoryGate\[Ellipsis] of \
LongShortTermMemoryLayer; the ports State and CellState are h and c, and are \
reached through NetGraph (sqWithStates).\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"55e97a0a-6006-4359-bb2f-fc28d20d84d2"],

Cell["2. The deep LSTM encoder and the thought vector", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"d0f496b7-3c0c-4f88-9d74-4f577c9fa6cd"],

Cell["\<\
The paper stacks four LSTMs: layer k reads the hidden states of layer k\
\[Minus]1. After the last source word, the final (h, c) of every layer \
together form a fixed-size summary of the whole sentence. The decoder starts \
from it.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"ec739c5a-8e9b-4eac-9401-2832ecc46f71"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["PASS  ",
  FontWeight->Bold,
  FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0.5, 0]],
 "a 3-layer stack coded from arrays matches NetChain of three \
LongShortTermMemoryLayers"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"a11af8e5-45c8-42c9-800c-7d045e1d9592"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["PASS  ",
  FontWeight->Bold,
  FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0.5, 0]],
 "the thought vector of the paper's model has 2 \[Times] 4 \[Times] 1000 = \
8000 numbers (paper, Section 3.4)"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"69758ac5-b3df-4825-97d2-0faac40c5d7e"],

Cell["3. The decoder step", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"3f24df63-e931-4b4e-907f-ecd585aa4482"],

Cell["\<\
The decoder is a second deep LSTM with its own weights (the paper uses two \
different LSTMs). It starts from the encoder's final states, reads the \
embedding of the previous output word, and a softmax over the target \
vocabulary gives the probability of the next word.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"730ec9f3-dc69-49a5-9281-ffbcbf115ea8"],

Cell["\<\
p(y_t | y_<t, x) = softmax(W\[CenterDot]h_t + b),   h_t from the top decoder \
layer\
\>", "Program",ExpressionUUID->"848e3e4b-cba2-47de-aef1-b2962f6c6b78"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["PASS  ",
  FontWeight->Bold,
  FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0.5, 0]],
 "the decoder's next-word distribution is a probability distribution over the \
10 target words"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"aa115572-c3e9-4faa-950a-cafcfeeb94fd"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["PASS  ",
  FontWeight->Bold,
  FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0.5, 0]],
 "the decoder's first layer, started from the encoder's states, matches \
LongShortTermMemoryLayer with State and CellState given"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"224af5cd-9c6a-458b-bc80-20fdeb60d7be"],

Cell["4. Reversing the source sentence", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"28eec756-f5af-4808-9582-4cbc8e7e0b54"],

Cell["\<\
Instead of mapping a, b, c to \[Alpha], \[Beta], \[Gamma], the LSTM maps c, \
b, a to \[Alpha], \[Beta], \[Gamma]. Put the source words at positions 1\
\[Ellipsis]T and the target words at T+1\[Ellipsis]2T, with target word i \
aligned to source word i. The table gives the distance between each aligned \
pair for T = 8.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"359dddd4-bddf-47d1-ad0c-e98cc7368cab"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TagBox[GridBox[{
    {"\<\"pair i\"\>", "1", "2", "3", "4", "5", "6", "7", "8"},
    {"\<\"forward\"\>", "8", "8", "8", "8", "8", "8", "8", "8"},
    {"\<\"reversed\"\>", "1", "3", "5", "7", "9", "11", "13", "15"}
   },
   AutoDelete->False,
   GridBoxBackground->{"Columns" -> {
       GrayLevel[0.85]}, "Rows" -> {{None}}},
   GridBoxFrame->{"Columns" -> {{True}}, "Rows" -> {{True}}},
   GridBoxItemSize->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{Automatic}}}],
  "Grid"]], "Output",ExpressionUUID->"7d6e4eab-3c65-4807-b541-4de8f9f492d9"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["PASS  ",
  FontWeight->Bold,
  FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0.5, 0]],
 "reversal leaves the average distance unchanged (8)"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"c4d1f8be-c954-4104-a386-3628bd7a5231"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["PASS  ",
  FontWeight->Bold,
  FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0.5, 0]],
 "but cuts the shortest distance from T = 8 to 1, so the first words can \
'communicate' at once"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"ddf53a29-5eae-4670-9ba9-e3d9cbe43926"],

Cell["\<\
The paper reports test perplexity falling from 5.8 to 4.7 and BLEU rising \
from 25.9 to 30.6 with reversed sources (Section 3.3).\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"6bc2dd81-8171-4731-aa70-0111aa52e8e3"],

Cell["5. Uniform initialisation in [\[Minus]0.08, 0.08]", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"0c2957d1-6a06-4069-aa81-7cb7bd016368"],

Cell["\<\
All parameters start uniformly distributed in [\[Minus]0.08, 0.08] (Section \
3.4). In Wolfram, NetInitialize does this with one option, except for the \
LSTM forget-gate biases, which it always sets to 1 (a later practice: \
Jozefowicz, Zaremba and Sutskever, 2015). sqUniformInit resets those too.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"7d473ed5-4217-4027-9ff4-67fcf00ddb86"],

Cell["\<\
NetInitialize[net, All, Method -> {\"Random\", \"Weights\" -> \
UniformDistribution[{-0.08, 0.08}], \"Biases\" -> UniformDistribution[{-0.08, \
0.08}]}]
then NetReplacePart[net, {k, \"ForgetGateBiases\"} -> \
RandomVariate[UniformDistribution[{-0.08, 0.08}], n]]\
\>", "Program",ExpressionUUID->"d5794c7d-e5e6-430f-800c-801e2695710f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["PASS  ",
  FontWeight->Bold,
  FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0.5, 0]],
 "NetInitialize alone sets the forget-gate biases to 1, so the paper's rule \
needs one more step"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"72420e3c-686a-4db4-b9cd-1d1d79c6f7f7"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["PASS  ",
  FontWeight->Bold,
  FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0.5, 0]],
 "after sqUniformInit, every one of the 5760 parameters of a 2-layer LSTM \
lies in [\[Minus]0.08, 0.08]"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"33df8507-2d3c-4a9e-b237-59e829bbce54"],

